1
00:00:00,024 --> 00:00:02,603
Speaker 2: At the National
Security Agency, the reasoning

2
00:00:02,603 --> 00:00:03,954
was strikingly literal.

3
00:00:04,784 --> 00:00:08,843
Photography and audio recording devices
of any kind are strictly forbidden

4
00:00:08,863 --> 00:00:10,594
inside the agency's facilities.

5
00:00:11,354 --> 00:00:14,934
And because this toy appeared to learn
new words from whatever it heard around

6
00:00:14,934 --> 00:00:20,323
it, officials worried it might be doing
exactly that, quietly recording snippets

7
00:00:20,323 --> 00:00:24,514
of classified conversations somewhere
inside its plastic shell and repeating

8
00:00:24,514 --> 00:00:28,993
them back later in its high-pitched
voice in a hallway or a parking lot far

9
00:00:28,994 --> 00:00:30,763
from where the words were first spoken.

10
00:00:31,723 --> 00:00:36,073
It sounds almost funny in hindsight,
and at the time, it was treated as a

11
00:00:36,073 --> 00:00:40,144
serious enough concern that officers
were told to remove the toys immediately.

12
00:00:40,343 --> 00:00:41,974
No exceptions and no debate.

13
00:00:42,464 --> 00:00:45,623
The same way anyone would be told
to remove an actual recording

14
00:00:45,624 --> 00:00:48,544
device found sitting on a desk
inside a classified building.

15
00:00:49,643 --> 00:00:53,753
Tiger Electronics moved quickly to shut
the story down before it grew any further.

16
00:00:54,663 --> 00:00:58,074
Roger Shiffman told reporters
directly that the toy had, in

17
00:00:58,074 --> 00:01:02,423
his words, "absolutely no ability
to do any recording whatsoever."

18
00:01:03,343 --> 00:01:08,053
Dave Hampton, one of the original
inventors, went further, demonstrating

19
00:01:08,054 --> 00:01:11,334
publicly that the toy's built-in
microphone was only sensitive enough

20
00:01:11,344 --> 00:01:15,043
to detect simple sound patterns,
monotonous beeps and claps.

21
00:01:15,664 --> 00:01:19,823
Nothing close to capturing and later
replaying full sentences of human speech.

22
00:01:20,594 --> 00:01:24,123
The truth was almost anticlimactic
compared to the fear it caused.

23
00:01:25,023 --> 00:01:28,593
Inside every one of these toys sat
a simple processor paired with a

24
00:01:28,603 --> 00:01:32,733
basic speech synthesis chip, no
more sophisticated than what powered

25
00:01:32,783 --> 00:01:34,824
other talking toys of that same era.

26
00:01:35,503 --> 00:01:37,893
The kind of components a
hobbyist could take apart on a

27
00:01:37,903 --> 00:01:39,473
kitchen table in an afternoon.

28
00:01:40,463 --> 00:01:43,183
The vocabulary was not learned
from the environment at all.

29
00:01:43,893 --> 00:01:48,183
It was hard-coded from the very start,
programmed to gradually swap out the

30
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:52,183
made-up language for English on a
fixed internal schedule, regardless

31
00:01:52,183 --> 00:01:55,803
of what anyone actually said to it
or whether anyone spoke to it at all.

32
00:01:56,643 --> 00:01:59,863
The toy was not listening and
adapting the way a person or even

33
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:01,413
a real recording device would.

34
00:02:02,103 --> 00:02:05,823
It was running a script it had been
given the day it left the factory and

35
00:02:05,823 --> 00:02:09,043
simply revealing more of that script
the longer it stayed powered on.

36
00:02:09,653 --> 00:02:13,723
Faced with the manufacturer's public
denials and a fairly simple explanation of

37
00:02:13,734 --> 00:02:19,804
the hardware sitting inside a thirty-five
dollar toy, the ban did not last long The

38
00:02:19,804 --> 00:02:24,073
National Security Agency quietly walked
it back rather than run a full technical

39
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:27,964
teardown to prove what was already fairly
obvious to anyone who opened one up.

40
00:02:28,923 --> 00:02:33,143
For a brief moment, a mass-market
Christmas toy had outmaneuvered one of the

41
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:37,703
most secretive government agencies in the
country, not through any actual capability

42
00:02:37,703 --> 00:02:41,993
hidden inside it, but purely through how
convincingly it pretended to have one.

43
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:45,593
The mania that made this story
possible eventually cooled

44
00:02:45,594 --> 00:02:47,413
the way every toy craze does.

45
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:51,393
Production of the original version
wound down by around two thousand

46
00:02:51,393 --> 00:02:56,183
and two after that run of more than
forty million units sold worldwide.

47
00:02:57,163 --> 00:03:02,194
But this was not the end of the toy's
story, only the end of its first act.

48
00:03:03,003 --> 00:03:07,064
In two thousand and five, Hasbro,
which had acquired Tiger Electronics

49
00:03:07,064 --> 00:03:11,593
along the way, brought the toy back
with new, more expressive features.

50
00:03:12,543 --> 00:03:16,043
Another version arrived in twenty
twelve with animated screen eyes

51
00:03:16,043 --> 00:03:17,813
instead of the old mechanical ones.

52
00:03:18,883 --> 00:03:22,293
A more connected version
followed in twenty sixteen, able

53
00:03:22,293 --> 00:03:23,773
to link to a smartphone app.

54
00:03:24,603 --> 00:03:28,664
And in twenty twenty-three, a fifth
generation model launched with

55
00:03:28,673 --> 00:03:32,224
voice activation and more than
six hundred built-in responses.

56
00:03:32,933 --> 00:03:37,363
By twenty twenty-five, the toy had
become a finalist for the National

57
00:03:37,363 --> 00:03:41,904
Toy Hall of Fame, more than a quarter
century after it first frightened a spy

58
00:03:41,904 --> 00:03:43,793
agency into writing a memo about it.

59
00:03:44,533 --> 00:03:48,353
So what actually happened in that strange
month in early nineteen ninety-nine?

60
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:52,533
Nobody built a working listening
device disguised as a children's toy.

61
00:03:53,203 --> 00:03:57,203
What actually happened is that two
inventors built something so convincingly

62
00:03:57,203 --> 00:04:02,023
alive that it fooled the exact people
whose entire job was not to be fooled.

63
00:04:02,903 --> 00:04:07,343
The National Security Agency spends its
existence trying to tell real signals from

64
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:10,113
noise, real threats from harmless ones.

65
00:04:10,874 --> 00:04:15,303
And for a few weeks, a toy powered by
a chip simpler than a pocket calculator

66
00:04:15,304 --> 00:04:19,143
managed to look like a signal worth
taking seriously at the highest

67
00:04:19,143 --> 00:04:20,803
levels of American intelligence.

68
00:04:21,583 --> 00:04:24,584
That is the part of this
story that outlasts the joke.

69
00:04:25,493 --> 00:04:28,493
The toy never needed a
microphone that actually worked.

70
00:04:29,153 --> 00:04:32,333
It only needed to seem like it might
and to seem like it was getting

71
00:04:32,333 --> 00:04:34,404
smarter every day you spent with it.

72
00:04:35,323 --> 00:04:38,814
Decades before anyone worried about
a smart speaker listening in the next

73
00:04:38,814 --> 00:04:42,503
room or a phone that seemed to know
what you were about to search for before

74
00:04:42,503 --> 00:04:47,193
you typed it, a fuzzy plastic owl with
blinking eyes had already taught an

75
00:04:47,193 --> 00:04:51,913
entire country, including its most
guarded agency, the same lesson those

76
00:04:51,914 --> 00:04:54,104
later devices would teach all over again.

77
00:04:54,774 --> 00:04:57,594
Something does not have to
actually understand you to make you

78
00:04:57,594 --> 00:05:00,253
believe completely that it does.

79
00:05:00,804 --> 00:05:03,683
The National Security Agency
eventually got its building back to

80
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:07,404
normal, and Furby went on to sell
tens of millions more units under

81
00:05:07,404 --> 00:05:11,193
a company that never once needed to
prove the toy was actually listening.

82
00:05:12,043 --> 00:05:14,154
It only ever needed everyone to wonder.
